foreign  [Music]  for Monday November 21st I'm Zoe Thomas  for The Wall Street Journal  over two dozen workers at meta have been  fired or disciplined as part of an  internal investigation by company  Executives into the misuse of an  internal system known as oops  that's according to people familiar with  the matter and documents viewed by The  Wall Street Journal the abuse of that  system raises questions about how much  control and access some meta staff and  contractors have to users data joining  us to discuss this wsj exclusive is our  reporter Kirsten grind hi Kirsten thanks  for coming on the show thanks so much  for having me can you start by telling  us a little bit more about this oops  system yes it's short for online  operations and it's this internal meta  system that allows employees and  contractors to basically file an  internal request almost like a complaint  when there's a problem with their or  like a friend or family's Facebook or  Instagram account one way to think of it  is like a concierge helpline right if  you lost your password if your account  has been hacked but again only for  Insider at meta and only for their  friends and family what do we know about  how the system was misused  what meta has been investigating for  over a year is this issue where through  the oops Channel if you're an employee  or a contractor you would have access to  a user's account which you can do  through that channel and so rather than  using that to say help a user some of  these employees and contractors were  misusing it they were getting into user  accounts often with the purpose of  taking the accounts over and reselling  them sometimes for a lot of money can  you tell us a little bit more about how  we know this information  so my colleague and I Bob McMillan we've  actually been looking into this for  quite a long time because we kind of  couldn't believe that actual employees  and contractors some of whom were  physical security guards so not really  people who normally would have access to  meta systems that this was happening and  so we spent a lot of time calling around  and talking to various parties and we  learned through the course of that that  this was actually an issue that meta has  been investigating for some time you  know for the people who were victims of  the misuse of this oop system what was  the experience like for them  so  meta is notorious for its terrible  customer service meaning they actually  have almost no customer service so  unfortunately there's a frequency in  which users either get locked out of  their account or are hacked or something  as simple as losing your password and  there's just literally no one you can  contact at meta by phone or email now  meta is working on this they say they're  committed to building this out but right  at this moment there's not anything like  that so for the user you actually have  no idea if it's an actual bad actor  hacker that's gotten into your account  or now maybe what we're revealing is it  could actually have been a meta employee  right now we're saying we know of more  than two dozen employees and contractors  over the last year so I don't want to  give the mistaken impression that  there's widespread misuse of the system  by meta employees but as a user you  would just basically be you know having  issues with your account that you can't  fix and in a lot of cases you just lose  your account because you can't get  through to anyone  what steps has meta taken to deal with  the situation  so as part of this investigation they've  actually fired and or disciplined we say  more than two dozen employees and  contractors and so they've been at this  for quite a long time looking into it  they're clearly aware it's an issue  they've told us that this is not  something that they will tolerate on any  of their platforms and that they'll  continue to go after these types of  people and you mentioned some of those  people were contractors what has the the  contracting company said about the  situation so the company is called  Allied Universal and they literally just  provide security guards stationed at  meta properties but the thing is  accessing this oops platform was kind of  a perk of the job and so what happened  there is like some people were abusing  it so Allied has told us they don't  stand for that and they won't tolerate  abuse either I mean what what is this  situation then tell us about kind of our  access and control of you know what we  think of as our own personal data and to  these social media pages that can be so  important to us personally or you know  even for some businesses I mean it's  true unfortunately that you can lose  access and not get it back there's ways  and we helpfully put something online at  the Wall Street Journal website to  protect your account from hackers and to  make sure you have the right Security on  there so you don't lose your account or  you don't find yourself in a situation  where you're trying to contact Facebook  or Instagram with no customer service  but at the end of the day you know it's  their platform and there's not a lot of  recourse at this point  for losing your account or anything like  that  all right that's our reporter Kirsten  Grine thanks so much for explaining this  to us thanks for having me  and that's it for today's Tech news  briefing for more Tech stories head over  to our website wsj.com  I'm Zoe Thomas for The Wall Street  Journal  thanks for listening  [Music]  [Music]

